3845 women, 390 had ≥1 marker. There were 10/3845 cases of DS; 3 were among patients with low-risk FTS (n = 3727). In 55
patients, NCR adjusted the risk from low to high without increasing detection rate. NCR did not modify risk to allow for
detection of the 3 DS among patients with low-risk FTS even though 2 of these fetuses had 1 minor marker each. There 
were 7 DS among patients with high-risk FTS (n = 118). Use of NCR increased positive predictive value from 7/118 (5.1%) 
to 7/53 (13.2%). CONCLUSION Screening for minor markers is useful in patients with high-risk FTS. It is of questionable 
benefit in patients with low-risk FTS."}]}                                                                              
